.TH CH1M3RA 1 "2026" "ch1m3ra 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
.B ch1m3ra \- packet sniffer written from scratch (no libpcap)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ch1m3ra
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ch1m3ra
captures and decodes network traffic using a raw
.BR AF_PACKET (7)
socket; no libpcap, scapy or Wireshark code is involved.  Every layer is parsed
by hand: Ethernet/VLAN, ARP, IPv4/IPv6 (including the extension-header chain),
TCP/UDP/ICMP, 802.11/radiotap management and control frames, and the application
protocols on
top (DNS, TLS, QUIC/HTTP3, HTTP/1.x, HTTP/2, DoH/DoT, SMB2, RDP, LDAP, Kerberos).
When given a monitor-mode interface it detects the link-layer type automatically
and decodes the radiotap frames live.
.PP
It can capture live, read and write
.B pcap
and
.B pcapng
files, filter in the kernel with classic BPF, reassemble IPv4/IPv6 fragments and
TCP streams, track UDP flows, fingerprint TLS clients (JA3/JA4), harvest
cleartext credentials and hostnames, and drive a full-screen terminal TUI.  A set
of active offensive tools (ARP poisoning, SYN scanning, DHCP starvation, frame
replay) can also transmit hand-built frames.
.PP
Live capture needs root, or the
.B cap_net_raw
and
.B cap_net_admin
capabilities (see
.BR setcap (8)).
Reading a file with
.B \-r
needs no privileges.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Capture source
.TP
.B \-i " iface"
Capture on
.IR iface .
Default: all interfaces.
.TP
.B \-l
List interfaces (address, state, MAC) and exit.
.TP
.B \-r " file"
Read packets from a
.IR file .
Auto-detects classic pcap and pcapng.
.TP
.B \-Z
Zero-copy capture using a
.B PACKET_RX_RING
.TP
.B \-M
Do not put the interface into promiscuous mode.
.SS Output
.TP
.B \-w " file"
Write captured packets to
.IR file .
With
.B \-r
this filters one capture into another.  A
.B .pcapng
extension selects the pcapng container, otherwise classic pcap is written.
.TP
.B \-W " MB"
With
.BR \-w ,
rotate to
.IB base \-NNNNN .ext
once the current file exceeds
.I MB
megabytes.
.TP
.B \-\-rotate-secs " n"
With
.BR \-w ,
rotate every
.I n
seconds of packet time (works offline too).
.TP
.B \-o " fmt"
Output format:
.BR text " (default), " json ", " ndjson ", " csv ", " zeek .
.B json
and
.B ndjson
emit one object per line (ndjson adds a
.B "schema" : 1
field);
.B csv
emits a header row plus one row per packet;
.B zeek
emits a TSV log with a
.B #fields
header (path
.IR ch1m3ra ).
.TP
.B \-e
Relative timestamps: prepend a
.B +seconds
column measured from the first printed packet.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: one line per packet, no details or payload dump.
.TP
.B \-B " len"
Dump up to
.I len
payload bytes (default 96,
.B 0
disables the dump).
.TP
.B \-C
Disable colors.
.TP
.BI \-\-load\-config " file"
Apply settings from a config file (simple
.BI key " = " value
lines) before any other flag, so explicit command-line flags win.
.B \-
or
.B auto
means
.IR ~/.ch1m3rarc .
.TP
.B \-\-save\-config
Write the current settings to
.I ~/.ch1m3rarc
and exit.
.SS Filtering
.TP
.B \-P " proto"
Keep only this protocol: tcp, udp, icmp, icmpv6, arp, ip, ipv6, dns, tls,
http, frag, ... (\fB\-P\fP is the userspace, fully-parsed filter).
.TP
.B \-H " host"
Keep packets whose source/destination address contains
.IR host ,
whose ARP sender/target matches, or whose decoded DNS question name, TLS SNI or
HTTP Host header matches.
.TP
.B \-p " port"
Keep packets with this source or destination port.
.TP
.B \-F " expr"
Kernel BPF filter.  A tcpdump-like expression is compiled to classic BPF and
attached with
.BR SO_ATTACH_FILTER ,
so the kernel drops non-matching packets before they reach userspace.  The same
program runs through a built-in interpreter in
.B \-r
mode.
.IP
Grammar:
.BI tcp " ... " dns ,
.BI port " N",
.BI src | dst " port " N ,
.BI host " A.B.C.D",
.BI host " 2001:db8::1",
.BI src | dst " host " ... ,
.BR and / && ,
.BR or / || ,
.BR not / ! ,
parentheses, and implicit
.B and
between terms.  Extended terms:
.BR vlan ,
.BI ether " [src|dst] host " mac ,
.BI greater " N" " / " less " N"
(packet length),
.BI len " > N" " / " len " < N" ,
and
.BI tcp[tcpflags]
with an optional
.BI & " FLAGS"
mask and an optional
.BI = " N" " / " "!= 0"
comparison.
.IP
Fast-path trade-off: offsets assume untagged Ethernet frames without IPv6
extension headers, so
.B port
tests shift out of view on those frames.  Use
.B \-P
/
.B \-p
(userspace) or add
.B \-E
for full parsing.
.TP
.B \-E
Thorough filtering: run
.B \-F
through the userspace interpreter even live, so it sees fully parsed frames
(VLAN tags and IPv6 extension headers included) at the cost of copying every
frame up first.
.TP
.B \-n " count"
Stop after
.I count
packets.
.TP
.B \-s " len"
Snapshot length: bytes captured per packet (default 65535).
.SS Decoding and analysis
.TP
.B \-A
Reassemble IPv4/IPv6 fragments.  When a datagram is complete it is decoded as a
whole (TCP/UDP/app layer); incomplete fragments stay shown as
.BR IP-FRAG .
.TP
.BI \-T " id"
Follow a TCP stream: print the reassembled payload as text and suppress packet
lines.
.B 0
follows every stream; ids appear as
.B #N
on packet lines and in the stats.
.TP
.B \-D
Defensive anomaly detection.  Passive detectors for ARP spoofing (a changing
MAC/IP binding, or one MAC claiming many addresses), ICMPv6 Neighbor
Advertisement spoofing, rogue DHCP (more than one server on the segment), name
poisoning (mDNS/LLMNR/NBNS answers binding a name to two hosts, plus WPAD
takeover), VLAN double-tagging and Cisco DTP frames, and 802.11 rogue-AP (the
same SSID advertised by two BSSIDs) and deauthentication floods.  Each distinct
anomaly is reported once to stderr and counted in the stats.  Nothing is
transmitted.
.TP
.B \-\-follow-out " file"
With
.BR \-T ,
write the followed stream's reassembled payloads as a synthetic
Ethernet/IP/TCP pcap that can be reopened in Wireshark.
.TP
.B \-U
Interactive TUI (needs a tty on stdout).  Keys:
.B s
start,
.B x
or space stop,
.B j
/
.B k
scroll (also
.B PgUp
/
.B PgDn
and
.BR Ctrl\-U / Ctrl\-D ),
.B g
/
.B G
jump,
.B Enter
toggle the detail pane for the selected packet (then
.B [
/
.B ]
scroll it),
.B /
search (then
.B n
/
.B N
for the next/previous match),
.B p
switch pane (packets / hits / hosts / talkers),
.B c
clear,
.B f
follow all streams,
.B q
quit.
The packet view also scrolls with the mouse wheel or the arrow keys (SGR mouse
reporting is enabled while the TUI is up).  The dashboard is built from a title
row, a metric row (captured/shown/filtered/malformed counts), an "elite" row
(elapsed time, average and current packet rate, harvested hit/cred/host badges
and a per-second packet-rate sparkline), an optional active-filter row, the
per-packet log with a column header and a scrollbar, a key-hint row and a status
bar.  On terminals at least 100 columns wide the packets view adds a right-hand
side panel with a protocol-mix histogram, the top talkers and a harvest summary.
Rows are coloured by protocol and the detail pane shows a sectioned decode
(L2/L3/L4, application layer, harvested credentials, payload hexdump).
.TP
.B \-K
Harvest cleartext credentials and tokens from payloads (HTTP Basic/Bearer and
form bodies, FTP, POP3, IMAP, SMTP, Telnet, SNMP, cookies, JWTs) plus service
banners and the structured SMB2/RDP/LDAP/Kerberos fields.  TLS is never
decrypted.
.TP
.B \-k " file"
Like
.BR \-K ,
and append each hit to
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-O
Passive OS fingerprinting from each host's initial TTL and TCP options
(p0f-style).
.TP
.B \-Y
Top-talkers: rank hosts and conversations by bytes.
.TP
.B \-N
Learn hostnames from mDNS, LLMNR, NBNS and DNS responses.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help page.
.SS Active (offensive) tools
These build Ethernet frames by hand and transmit them on an
.B AF_PACKET
socket.  They require root (or
.BR cap_net_raw )
and an explicit flag; use them only on networks you own or are authorised to
test.  They fail fast with
.B "socket(AF_PACKET): Operation not permitted"
when run unprivileged.
.TP
.BI \-\-arp\-poison " victim,gateway[,iface]"
ARP-spoof the pair to MITM their traffic.  On
.B Ctrl-C
the true MAC/IP bindings are re-asserted.
.TP
.BI \-\-syn\-scan " host[:ports]"
Half-open TCP port scan.  Use
.I iface
via
.BR \-i .
Ports are given as
.B 22,80,443
or
.BR 1\-1024 .
Reports open/closed/filtered and RSTs half-open sockets.
.TP
.BI \-\-banner\-grab " host[:ports]"
Full-connect probe that reads the first bytes each service sends (SSH, SMTP,
FTP, IMAP, ...), nudging HTTP with a
.B HEAD
request.  Use
.B \-\-banner\-timeout " ms"
to set the read timeout (default 1500).  Unlike the raw tools this uses the
kernel TCP stack, so it follows normal routing.
.TP
.BI \-\-vlan\-hop " iface:outer:inner"
Send 802.1ad QinQ double-tagged frames (outer S-tag
.IR outer ,
inner C-tag
.IR inner )
to test for VLAN-hopping exposure.  Ctrl-C stops it.
.TP
.BI \-\-dhcp\-starve " iface[:count]"
Flood a DHCP server with DISCOVERs using random client MACs to exhaust the pool.
.TP
.BI \-\-deauth " bssid[,client][,count]"
Inject 802.11 deauthentication frames over a monitor\-mode interface given with
.BR \-i .
The client defaults to broadcast and the count to forever (Ctrl-C stops it).
Authorised testing only.
.TP
.BI \-\-beacon\-flood " ssid[,count]"
Flood the air with fake\-AP beacons carrying
.I ssid
and a fresh random BSSID each frame, over a monitor\-mode interface.  Authorised
testing only.
.TP
.BI \-\-replay " pcap"
Re-send frames from a pcap on the interface given with
.BR \-i .
.TP
.BI \-\-rewrite\-src " ip"
With
.BR \-\-replay ,
replace the source IPv4 address and fix the header checksum.
.TP
.BI \-\-bench " n"
Decode the pcap given with
.B \-r
.I n
times through the full decode pipeline and report packets per second and
nanoseconds per packet.  Useful for sizing the decoder against a link rate.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Live capture on one interface:
.B sudo ch1m3ra \-i wlan0
.TP
Capture only TLS:
.B sudo ch1m3ra \-i eth0 \-P tcp \-p 443
.TP
Zero-copy capture with a kernel filter:
.B sudo ch1m3ra \-i eth0 \-Z \-F 'tcp and port 443'
.TP
Interactive dashboard with harvesting and fingerprinting:
.B sudo ch1m3ra \-i wlan0 \-K \-O \-U
.TP
JSON to jq:
.B ch1m3ra \-r capture.pcap \-o json | jq \-r 'select(.app=="dns") | .dns.qname'
.TP
Inspect streams, then follow one:
.B ch1m3ra \-r capture.pcap \-q
.br
.B ch1m3ra \-r capture.pcap \-T 3
.TP
Convert a pcap to pcapng:
.B ch1m3ra \-r in.pcap \-w out.pcapng
.TP
Rotating capture files:
.B sudo ch1m3ra \-i eth0 \-w cap.pcapng \-W 100 \-\-rotate\-secs 3600
.TP
Grab service banners:
.B ch1m3ra \-\-banner\-grab 10.0.0.1:22,80,8080
.TP
Test VLAN-hopping exposure with double-tagged frames:
.B sudo ch1m3ra \-i eth0 \-\-vlan\-hop eth0:1:100
.SH FILES
.TP
.I tests/check.sh
assertion suite (
.B make test
).
.TP
.I tests/gate.sh
behavior gate against the frozen baseline (
.B make gate
).
.TP
.I tests/netns.sh
live-capture and injection harness (
.BR "make netns" ;
needs root, skips otherwise).
.SH NOTES
Only capture or inject traffic on networks you own or are authorized to test.
The active tools put real frames on the wire.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tcpdump (8),
.BR tshark (1),
.BR ip (8),
.BR setcap (8)
